course: dictionary
lesson: 6466
title: National, the whole country
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: National means belonging to, or shared by, a whole country, not just one part of it. «Waitangi Day, 6 February, is its national day.» That day belongs to all of New Zealand, so national means everyone in the country shares it.
explain: Check the words around it. A national park protects nature across a country, and a national anthem is sung by the whole nation. If you swap in "for the whole country" and the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
explain: National can also mean owned or run by the government. «The capital was put outside every state so that no single state would control the national government.» Here national government means the government of the whole country. As a noun, a national is a citizen, so «a British national» is someone who is a citizen of Britain.
fact: National means belonging to, or shared by, a whole country.
fact: A national park protects nature and wildlife, like Yellowstone or the Lake District.
fact: A national symbol, such as a flag, an anthem or an animal, is shared by everyone in a country.
fact: «Switzerland has four national languages, German, French, Italian and Romansh.» Here national means used across the whole country.
fact: As a noun, a national is a citizen of a particular country.
exercise: «Waitangi Day, 6 February, is its national day.» What does national mean here? | *Shared by the whole country | Only for one town | Happening in the summer | Owned by one family
exercise: «Ashoka's lion pillar is today a national emblem of India.» What is a national emblem? | A type of Indian cooking | A small stone for a garden | A map of one city | *A symbol shared by the whole country
exercise: «A British national» is a person. What does national mean in this phrase? | A British king | A sports player | *A citizen of Britain | A type of train
test: «Switzerland has four national languages.» What does national mean here? | *Used across the whole country | Written only in old books | Spoken only by children | Spoken only in one small village
test: «The national museum is run by the government.» What does national mean here? | *Owned or run by the government of the country | Painted by children | Owned by a single family | Open only at night
exam: Nadia says national means for the whole country, not just one part. Which example fits best? | a school trip to the nearby town | a local shop that sells bread | *a national holiday like Canada Day | a park in my street
